{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/59151-questions-about-play-on-words-exploit","id":59151,"title":"Questions about \"Play on Words\" Exploit","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-27T14:39:21-08:00","id":59151,"title":"Questions about \"Play on Words\" Exploit","content":"Does the statement used for &quot;Play on Words&quot; need to be declarative, or can a question be used as well? For instance (and citing the book's &quot;Bear arms&quot; example as a precedent), could &quot;Are you chickening out?&quot; turn a person into some sort of bizarre chicken-man?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-27T22:35:47-08:00","id":59523,"title":"","content":"I would say declarative since it's looking to be meant to work on whomever made the multiple-meaning statement. If the subject is the second person (ie, &quot;you&quot;) then the meaning is shifted a bit. Now, if the person had said &quot;Are you saying I'm chicken/chickening-out?&quot; then I'd say fair game.<br /> <br /> Relatedly, if the second person had responded with &quot;Yes, I'm chickening out&quot; then I would say the power applies on the second person, but if it's a simple &quot;Damn straight&quot; then no dice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2059-jotun","name":"Jotun","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2059_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T09:26:43-08:00","id":60837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Demon: the Descent, page 171</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Note: This Exploit is challenging to use. <b>It requires that the player pay attention to what other characters are saying and jump on the opportunity to activate the power.</b><br /> <br /> [...]<ul><li>Incapacity: The demon shifts meaning to inconvenience or incapacitate a character (the <b>Spanish-speaking victim says \u201c<i>estoy esposado</i>\u201d \u2014 \u201cI am married\u201d </b>\u2014 but winds up in handcuffs). [...]</li> <li>General Strangeness: <b>The victim says that he will</b> \u201cwrite\u201d something down, only to wind up performing a \u201crite.\u201d Another refers to the right to \u201cbear\u201d arms and <b>looks down to discover his shirt sleeves missing</b> (\u201cbare\u201d arms), or that they have grown thick fur and claws (\u201cbear\u201d arms).</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div>(emphasis mine)<br /> <br /> From these, it seems that you can only use it on someone with something they said.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Once is happenstance. Twice is coincidence. Three times is enemy action. Four times is Infrastructure.<br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> Rule #2: Never let the game-mechanics get in the way of a good story.","edited":{"userid":1642,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1642-paddon","user":"Paddon","date":". 01-29-2014, 12:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1642-paddon","name":"Paddon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1642_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"326"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T01:10:34-08:00","id":62043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/59151-questions-about-play-on-words-exploit#post59151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does the statement used for &quot;Play on Words&quot; need to be declarative, or can a question be used as well? For instance (and citing the book's &quot;Bear arms&quot; example as a precedent), could &quot;Are you chickening out?&quot; turn a person into some sort of bizarre chicken-man?</div> </div> </div> </div>All of the examples in the power's description indicate that the victim of the power must &quot;say&quot;. One example goes so far as to say &quot;unless the drinker actually uses the word 'shot'.&quot; where the drinker is the target of the power. None of these powers are questions.<br /> <br /> If I had to comment on the intent of the power, I would say that the power relies upon the individual having taken that action or committing to take the action (in the I will write it down example). The question &quot;Are you chickening out?&quot; does not have the victim committing an act or intending to commit an act. <br /> <br /> &quot;Go take a break&quot; (invalid, target does not commit to taking a break, no action by the target to affect)<br /> &quot;I will take a break&quot; (valid, target has stated he intends to take an action. That action may be affected)<br /> <br /> Regarding the bear arms example, I believe the context of that is the &quot;I (or we) have the right to bear arms&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1930-rixx","name":"Rixx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1930_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T06:12:59-08:00","id":62124,"title":"","content":"The short story at the beginning of a chapter seems to have a lot of leeway with the power. A guy spots two demons disguised as woman. Ends up pissing them off, but coming back, apologizing, and saying something like &quot;I thought I would give it a shot...&quot; And ends up as if he was shot. <br /> <br /> I could understand the demon being shot there (giving her a shot through the heart), or the demon getting a drink (a shot of alcohol). But the speaker getting shot?<br /> <br /> <br /> That's a lot of twisting intent around there. Not sure how I feel about that. If that's how it works, someone just saying &quot;Hi&quot; can be flung up into the ceiling, then fall back down. &quot;Hey there&quot; can generate hay from nothing. Perversion powers when virtually anything can be a double entendre. With a cooperative PC party, it very quickly becomes a &quot;Do Anything&quot; power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T08:16:30-08:00","id":62183,"title":"","content":"<br /> &quot;.... 'I figured I'd take a shot -<br /> <br /> Something hit him in the chest. He looked down and saw blood gushing from a hole just to the left of his sternum. 'What-'<br /> <br /> The angry one leaned and grabbed his phone from the dashboard. 'You took a shot,' she said, 'Hope it works out for you.'&quot; pg. 283<br /> <br /> <br /> So he didn't say &quot;I thought I would give it a shot&quot;.<br /> According to the examples and the story he says he'd take a shot and then the demon uses the power and the man takes a shot. .<br /> <br /> <br /> In my view, simply saying &quot;hi&quot; is not enough to use the power to fling a person skyward. Why? the statement doesn't really refer back to the speaker.<br /> If the speaker said &quot;I'm so high right now&quot;, I could see that as an opportunity to fling the speaker skywards.<br /> <br /> As to hay there causing hay to be generated, I don't think it works that way but I'll get back to hey/hay in a bit.<br /> <br /> <br /> I like to think of it like this, the power has two targeting components<br /> 1) the individual speaking<br /> 2) the statement to be manipulated.<br /> <br /> **For a statement to damage the speaker the statement itself must also target the speaker<br /> Even if the statement can be manipulated, if it does not refer to the individual speaking the power cannot be used to harm the target.<br /> <br /> This explains the key difference between &quot;would give it a shot&quot; and &quot;figured I'd take a shot&quot;<br /> shot can be manipulated<br /> however in one example he GIVES a shot, and in the other he TAKES a shot.<br /> <br /> &quot;Hi&quot; yes, you can twist it to mean &quot;high&quot; but again, there's noting left in the comment to affect the speaker.<br /> &quot;I'm so high right now&quot; high can be twisted, The speaker can be affected because he is linked to his statement.<br /> contrast with<br /> &quot;You're so high right now.&quot; as above, high can be twisted, but the speaker is not referring to themselves so that statement cannot be used to hurl the speaker skyward.<br /> <br /> <br /> Though, I think that one could possibly use the power to compel someone to take some kind of action (the write/rite example) so maybe I could use &quot;give it a shot&quot; to compel the guy to shoot someone later or to buy someone a drink.<br /> <br /> This is where &quot;Hey there&quot; could be used. I think you could use the power to compel a person to bring hay, &quot;there&quot;. I disagree with the idea that the power can simply generate hay from somewhere into the vicinity, why? because you can twist Hey to mean hay, but fundamentally the target is the speaker, not a conjured pile of hay. In this way we see that the speaker must acquire hay and bring it 'there' provided that the task doesn't take more than successes + Primum hours as described in the power for non-damaging uses of the exploit.<br /> <br /> Is this power widely applicable? Yes<br /> Is it open to abuse? HELL YES.<br /> I would advise caution and conservative interpretations when using or adjudicating this power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1930,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1930-rixx","user":"Rixx","date":".  fixed some spelling, added a page number 01-31-2014, 11:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1930-rixx","name":"Rixx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1930_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T17:27:27-08:00","id":62596,"title":"","content":"You make some good points, but I seem to recall that, during the playtesting, 'Play on Words' was used to turn a shabby apartment complex called The Sterling Apartments into <i>sterling</i> apartments (clean, shiny, up-to-date, and in excellent condition), so I think it doesn't matter what the statement refers to, only that someone says something applicable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T18:46:53-08:00","id":62669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/59151-questions-about-play-on-words-exploit?p=62596#post62596\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You make some good points, but I seem to recall that, during the playtesting, 'Play on Words' was used to turn a shabby apartment complex called The Sterling Apartments into <i>sterling</i> apartments (clean, shiny, up-to-date, and in excellent condition), so I think it doesn't matter what the statement refers to, only that someone says something applicable.</div> </div> </div> </div>The apartments say they're sterling and so they are. :P<br /> <br /> The playtesting also included successfully using Freudian Slip, a power that does not work on demons, on Simon Flannery, a demon. Story is king.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T18:56:20-08:00","id":62680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/59151-questions-about-play-on-words-exploit?p=62669#post62669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Story is king.</div> </div> </div> </div>Truth.<br /> <br /> If it makes for better fiction, by all means make it happen. Exceptions can be neat! (boy who lived, bends all elements, uses more than one kind of magic, is actually an alien, etc., etc.,)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1930-rixx","name":"Rixx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1930_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}